also got a lot of great help from our  collaborator paul trapnell  the university of washington and i  mentioned during the talk the  contributions by a.j  and then finally i'd like to thank my  uh funding sources uh and my  institutions particularly the fred hutch  uw genome sciences and howard hughes uh  medical institute so uh  with that i'd be happy to uh answer any  questions  thank you dr bloom for your informative  presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar  if you have questions you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located to the far  left of your screen let's get started  our first question is what are the  advantages of using pac bio sequencing  over  oxford nano sorry nano pore sequencing  yeah so as i described in order to get  the single cell virus sequences we need  the long read sequencing technique to  get the the full sequence so you can't  use something like luminous sequencing  which is short v  so there are two uh long read  technologies at least i'm familiar with  pac bios circular consensus sequence and  oxford nanopore  sequencing and so the pac bios circular  consensus sequencing was super accurate  which made it extremely suited for  our purposes uh we got really high  accurate circular consensus sequences uh  particularly at the time we were doing  this i don't think they were comparable  high accuracy circular consensus  sequences approaches described for  oxford manafort  some are now being described we haven't  tested those out ourselves so it's  possible that you could do something  similar now with manafort sequencing  but we've just gotten such good results  with biosequencing that we're just  sticking with that  thank you the next question i have  coming in is the induction of type  i ifn in affected cells dependent on  virus subtype  avian or human origin on a single cell  level  yeah so that's a really great question  uh so the question  is essentially as i read it uh i mean  you could also ask about what happens in  cells of different species but the  the question as i read it is sort of uh  depending on whether you have  a human flu virus or an avian flu virus  would induce a interferon  in these cells differently we haven't  uh directly  done all the experiments uh to answer  that question  but i think the answer would be yes so  everything i was describing to you  in the work right there was with the wsn  virus which is a lab adapted strain of  influenza  there have been a number of other  studies uh  including a nice one with aj tavalthius  uh  by aj tobeltius looking at many viral  rnas and various other things  that suggest that avian adapted viruses  might  induce more interferon in human cells  than human adapted viruses and certainly  this would make sense from a  evolutionary or teleological perspective  you would expect that a virus  that is really well adapted to humans  might get very good  at evading the innate immune response in  human cells  and maybe a viruses coming from a bird  or some other species  might not be as good at doing all the  intricate  things it has to do to evade an innate  immune response in human cells but i  agree it would be  fascinating to look at this at the  single cell level  uh and we haven't done that yet but i  think that's a really great question and  i think something that would be  really valuable to do and that we and  i'm sure others are working on is really  sort of  compare uh the sort of dynamics of these  sorts of processes  uh across both different cell types and  horses isolated from different species  and there is uh it doesn't do it doesn't  exactly get to this question but there's  a nice recent paper  by anis lowen's group uh i'm  blanking right now on the first author  it's nate  that's his first name but i can't  remember his last name that that sort of  gets at this question with some single  cell studies and does show not  not particularly with respect to  interferon induction but with respect to  other aspects of viral replication some  evidence that  avian adapted viruses can be worse  sort of it undergoing single cell  replication  in human cells band can be adapted  viruses  so that's the thing  um the next question i have could this  approach be  applied to other viruses  uh yes i think it could uh  and certainly single cell  transcriptomics has now been applied to  some other viruses  i'm not aware of single cell virus  sequencing that's been applied  in this way to other viruses there are  some quasi similar things that have been  done in hiv  uh the key thing for the exact approach  that we were taking  is the virus has to produce  polyadenylated transcripts  because we were just using the standard  10x chromium three prime capture which  requires a  poly a tail for those illegal dt primers  to work ah  so for something like a flavi virus with  doesn't polyadenylate  its mrnas the exact approach that we use  wouldn't work  uh however there has been work included  by  steve quake and fabio zumini uh that can  kind of overcome some of these problems  using slightly  sequencing technologies that the if the  viral mrnas are not polyadenylated  another issue for the single cell virus  sequencing although things like back  biosequencing are great  there is a limit to how long you can do  a good circular consensus sequencing  both just a read length limit  and uh and sort of a  limit on how long something you can  reverse transcribe is  so something like a coronavirus which  has a 30kb viral genome  it's pretty hard to imagine capturing  all of that in sort of a single-pack  bioread you might have to  get around that problem somehow but i do  think this sort of  these basic questions and a lot of  aspects of these approaches  could be applied to other viruses and i  think that's a very promising thing  great the next question um is the cell  cycle stage at the time of infection  playing a role for the  viral excuse me from my pronunciation um  proliferation  yeah yeah that's that's a really good  question uh so  so sort of as i was mentioning we were  interested and i would consider cell  cycle sort of an example of  a pre-existing cellular state and you  can imagine that  cells are going to be at different  stages in the cell cycle uh there are  things you can do in these experiments  to synchronize the cells  at least if mom's sort of similar  cellular state importantly we could not  do that so the cells are going to be the  distribution of cellular  cycle stages and you can imagine that uh  that could play how well the viral  infection took off  because of the particular way we did  these experiments we're only sampling  the transcriptome  10 hours later so we have to sort of try  to use the transcript on the 10 hours  after the viruses run wild in the cell  to figure out what the cell state was at  the time of infection  this is sort of an imperfect process  we did do it we didn't find like really  strong associations with cell cycle  expression g cell cycle gene expression  and and  proliferation but as i said it's the way  we were looking at it  might not work because we're looking 10  hours later after viral infection  so the the key cell cycle genes could be  at different transcription levels and  so i think this is a really really good  question certainly in the context of  sort of certain other systems it's been  shown that  cell cycle stage at time of infection  could play a pretty important role  and i think a nice experiment would be  which we just haven't done would be to  synchronize the cells  and then do similar experiments and see  if that gives sort of  less variability in how well the virus  appropriates so i think that's  a good question sort of points to a  large area  that we haven't explored that could be  explored  got it we have time for a few more  questions um someone from the audience  is asking  for you to explain the intensity level  of the infectivity of the virus  yeah so i i actually i'm not like 100  sure what you mean  by that question so feel free to type  another question if i'm not  uh totally answering this but i think by  intensity level you might mean  multiplicity  of infections so how many virions are  going into individual cells  so in all of the experiments i showed  you the multiplicity of infection was  low it was on the order very i showed  it from a few different experiments very  a little bit from experiment to  experiment  it was on the order of 10 in terms of  transcriptionally active viral particles  not in terms of fully infectious virus  particles because we know that  fully infectious spiral particles are a  subset of transcriptionally active viral  particles  so that means most of the infected cells  are infected by just one varion  and maybe about 10 percent are infected  by multiple variants  uh we also grew our viral stock at low  multiplicity of infection and low  passage from reverse genetics rescue  so the variability in the viral  population is also pretty low  uh so hopefully that answers your  question by what you mean by intensity  level but if i'm not  understanding correctly what you mean by  intensity feel free to post another  question  and i can try to answer that better  great we're gonna wrap up with this  final question um are you surprised that  you saw  so few internal deletions in ifn  positive cells  yes this is another really good question  so in our inner ground positive cells  one of the types of viral defects we saw  were large internal deletions in the  polymerase but that wasn't the  most common thing that we saw and if  you've read a lot of the literature  you might know there's all this  literature on internal deletions that  interfere on induction  so why weren't we seeing more of it well  i think one factor is that we were using  this relatively pure low moi stock so it  didn't have a lot of internal deletions  but i think a bigger factor is that  until the types of experiments i was  describing  here most experiments people are doing  such as like running things out on gels  or looking perfect deletions and  segments  really only can see internal deletions  it can't see point mutations  so obviously if we were using a  technique that couldn't find any of the  point mutations we weren't doing the  single cell virus sequencing  the only thing we'd see is internal  deletions and from that  we might include that their internal  deletions are really important but  in practice they are important but we  can see that many other things are  equally if not more  thank you again dr bloom for your time  today and your important research  before we go i like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed  by the speaker via the contact  information that you provided at the  time of your registration  this webcast can also be viewed on  demand for six months until  march of 2021 and lab roots will alert  you via email when it's available for  replay and we encourage you to share  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's  live event until next time thank you  again dr bloom goodbye  thank you


